WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.020
Millie Bobby Brown is back on Netflix
and this time she's the delightfully smart

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:09.080
and witty younger sister of the world famous
Sherlock Holmes in an adventure that's set

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.230
to kick off a brand new franchise.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:14.690
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers,
I'm Jan and in this video we're diving into

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:18.640
the ending of Enola Holmes to examine all
the details and clues you might have missed

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:22.770
which set up the movie's sequel,
plus I'll reveal some easter eggs to the original

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:23.770
books.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:25.990
Obviously, spoilers ahead, so take care.

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:30.390
The ending of Enola Holmes sees the teenage
detective not only solve the case of the missing

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:34.200
marquess by herself and escape the clutches
of her controlling brother Mycroft,

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.260
but it also teases a potential team-up for
her and Sherlock.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:42.960
In the final few scenes, we get to see how
Enola and her brother think in a similar manner

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.340
and how each seems to playfully enjoy attempting
to outsmart the other.

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:51.920
When Enola finds a coded message in the newspaper,
she initially assumes it's from her missing

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.370
mother,
but quickly spots clues that reveal it's from

00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:58.210
Sherlock instead;
so she returns the trick by going to the meeting

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:02.010
place dressed as a boy and hiding herself
away from her brothers.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:06.560
However, Sherlock already suspects that Enola
will know he planted the message and senses

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:08.440
that she'll turn up anyway.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:12.810
And there's a clue that he probably spotted
her disguise when he walks through the colonnade

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:16.760
and glances to the right, then says
"Thought I saw something."

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:20.690
Sherlock continues walking, but I can't help
thinking that he deliberately decided to leave

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:24.390
Enola be and not let on to his brother Mycroft
what he saw.

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:28.970
After all, he already spotted the clue Enola
intentionally left for him, so he knew she

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:30.500
was there somewhere.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:35.010
In contrast to Mycroft, Sherlock seems to
have an understanding of who his sister really

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:38.030
is,
after all they both share a passion for detective

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:40.580
work,
and I think he's deciding to let Enola be

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:42.290
free if that's what she wants.

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:47.030
Still, I do expect we'll see the two of them
collaborate in some way in future sequels,

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:51.000
similar to how we already saw Sherlock mentoring
Enola earlier in the movie.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.460
"Look for what's there, not what you want
to be there.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:54.460
You'll see the truth soon enough."

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:58.092
And at the end of the film, he also asks Mycroft:
"If we do find her again, I'd like her to

00:01:58.092 --> 00:01:59.092
be my ward.

00:01:59.092 --> 00:02:00.092
I'll take care of her."

00:02:00.092 --> 00:02:03.600
In the original Enola Holmes novels, after
dodging and escaping her brothers for the

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:08.890
entire series, in the final book Enola and
Sherlock finally reunite and work together

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:09.890
to solve a case.

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:14.319
And I expect that in the movie franchise,
like the books, we'll see Enola working cases

00:02:14.319 --> 00:02:19.150
by herself for a while and perhaps crossing
paths with Sherlock from time to time.

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:23.439
She might even end up solving one of his cases,
getting ahead of him again like she did with

00:02:23.439 --> 00:02:24.840
the Tewksbury case.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:29.560
A full reunion of Enola and Sherlock too early
in the franchise wouldn't fit with the theme

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:32.400
of Enola being alone and making her own way
in the world,

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.430
but it could come later once she's fully grown
and more established.

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:41.340
Although Enola's mother Eudoria is absent
for much of the movie, her influence is still

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:42.969
felt throughout the story.

00:02:42.969 --> 00:02:47.659
For example, she was responsible for teaching
Enola reading, science, sports and martial

00:02:47.659 --> 00:02:50.310
arts,
things at the time traditionally only taught

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:53.159
to boys,
and she also imbued a philosophy in Enola

00:02:53.159 --> 00:02:55.019
that she should decide her own fate.

00:02:55.019 --> 00:02:57.189
"There are two paths you can take, Enola.

00:02:57.189 --> 00:02:59.620
Yours... or the path others choose for you."

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:03.480
When they reunite at the end of the film,
Eudoria explains that she left because she

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.640
wanted to change Enola's future,
and by that she means through her involvement

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:09.430
with the women's suffrage movement.

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:13.680
Eudoria isn't able to stay with her daughter
as she's clearly involved in activities that

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.029
make her a target for the authorities;
however, she thanks Enola for the irises and

00:03:18.029 --> 00:03:22.090
says if she ever needs her again she'll be
looking out for them, a reference to the secret

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:25.239
coded messages that Enola left for her mother
in the newspapers.

00:03:25.239 --> 00:03:29.170
"To find Mother, the first thing I need to
do is leave a cipher in every newspaper she

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:30.170
might read.

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:31.170
Are you blooming?

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:32.489
Send iris, please."

00:03:32.489 --> 00:03:36.089
This leaves things open for Eudoria to return
or not in a sequel,

00:03:36.089 --> 00:03:40.400
and that might depend on how much further
the filmmakers want to explore the beginnings

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:44.200
of the Women's Suffrage movement that are
touched upon in this first movie.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:49.189
The 1884 Reform Bill we see Tewksbury help
pass in the House of Lords with Enola's help

00:03:49.189 --> 00:03:53.160
of course, expanded voting rights in the UK
at the time.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:58.040
However, the bill failed to grant universal
suffrage, with women excluded entirely and

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:02.439
a further 40% of adult men excluded from any
voting rights.

00:04:02.439 --> 00:04:07.040
Given the prominence of this storyline in
the first film and Enola's budding independence

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.620
and activist mother,
I expect a sequel to further investigate the

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:14.659
restrictions placed on women in the late 19th
century.

00:04:14.659 --> 00:04:19.070
In contrast to the books, the movie decides
to develop a romantic connection between Enola

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:22.010
and Tewksbury, right after they fall off the
train.

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:26.530
By the end, this romance has bloomed to the
point where we see Enola place her hand on

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:28.510
his and then he kisses it.

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:33.470
Hopefully the sequels won't overdo the romance;
after all Enola turns down his offer of living

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:36.690
with him and his family,
and insists on living independently as she

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:39.910
clearly wants to set herself up as a professional
detective.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:44.930
Still, fans of the couple hoping for Tewksbury's
return in a sequel can look to Enola's words:

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:48.960
"You're not rid of me yet, Viscount Tewkesbury,
Marquess of Basilwether."

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:53.720
As for Enola's eldest brother Mycroft, he
says he's glad to be free of his responsibilities

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:56.460
for her when Sherlock asks to be her guardian
at the end.

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:00.650
I'm curious though to see if Mycroft will
continue to play an antagonistic role towards

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:02.320
our heroine in a sequel.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:07.000
He's clearly very reactionary in his views
towards women and society in general;

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:11.790
however, by the end of the book series, Mycroft
ends up very impressed by Enola's skills and

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:16.510
agrees to fund an education for her,
so it'll be interesting to see if, in time,

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:19.270
the movie franchise will redeem Mycroft in
a similar way.

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:21.260
"I am not the villain here."

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:25.820
At the end of the movie, as Enola concludes
the story, she tells us that she's "a detective,

00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:28.570
a decipherer, and a finder of lost souls."

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:32.590
The last point is a reference to the books
where Enola decides she's going to be what

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:35.240
she calls a "Scientific Perditorian".

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:40.240
The word "perditorian" is derived from the
Latin term "perditus" meaning "lost" and is

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:44.750
a made-up title in the novels where Enola
uses it to refer to herself as "a finder of

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:45.780
loved ones lost".

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:50.250
There are numerous missing persons cases in
the books that the movie sequels may adapt,

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:54.250
but one that's bound to prove interesting
is "The Case of the Bizarre Bouquets" in which

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:56.890
Sherlock's associate Dr Watson goes missing.

00:05:56.890 --> 00:06:01.640
I'll certainly be curious to see who's cast
as John Watson and how he might compare to

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:05.280
Martin Freeman's Watson from the BBC Sherlock
series.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:09.660
In the books, in order to pursue her vocation
of "Scientific Perditorian", Enola has to

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:14.500
masquerade as a secretary because in the late
19th century, she says, a scientist had to

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:15.500
be a man.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:19.530
So, for the movie sequels, it'll be interesting
to see whether Enola has to disguise herself

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:22.351
in the same way
or if she'll pretend to be a young man, as

00:06:22.351 --> 00:06:25.460
we've already seen her do, in order to pursue
her detective work.

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:29.930
And to learn a lot more about the fascinating
changes the Enola Holmes filmmakers made when

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:33.850
adapting the original book,
